terial without sufficient compensation since Black\'s king can escape to d7 or e7.\n\nThe most principled move is **Q: d1-h5**, putting maximum pressure on f7 and forcing Black to respond to the threat. This maintains the initiative and puts Black on the defensive.\n\nReason: Q: d1-h5 creates a double attack on f7 (along with my c4 bishop), forcing Black to weaken their kingside with g6 or block development with Qe7/Qf6. This maintains White\'s initiative and exploits Black\'s undeveloped position while keeping all my pieces.\n\nAnswer: Q: d1-h5'}
